08:30.055 --> 08:41.743
[SPEAKER_01]: motivation comes from, I've In a sense, did not take up an opportunity way back then, but now I want to make the most of it. I'm relatively

08:41.783 --> 08:53.148
[SPEAKER_01]: advanced in my age. I'm about 53 now, and I have this sense of... I would say calm urgency. But there is an urgency that there's only so much time,

08:53.168 --> 09:05.992
[SPEAKER_01]: and I would like to learn and explore as much as I can in that time. And yeah, I'm at it with all my interest and dedication. But I would say that

09:06.232 --> 09:17.674
[SPEAKER_01]: because I did somewhat of a rare animal, perhaps, in that I know a lot of my friends and other pilots who at some point of time in their careers

09:17.694 --> 09:27.877
[SPEAKER_01]: decided they've had enough and all they want to do is fly and they switch to flying. In my case, I was actually pretty much done with my career and I

09:27.897 --> 09:35.803
[SPEAKER_01]: wanted to move on to the next phase of my life. And I was just trying to find things to do. And I have lots of other hobbies and interests and

09:35.843 --> 09:47.234
[SPEAKER_01]: pursuits. But once I started paragliding, it immediately caught my fancy. And it checks just a lot of different boxes for me, and I'm sure for

09:47.274 --> 10:00.871
[SPEAKER_01]: many other pilots. And I just followed it. And just to give a quick recap of those boxes it takes, it gives a sense of purpose. For me, the sense of

10:00.891 --> 10:11.739
[SPEAKER_01]: purpose comes from just trying to understand and explore the world. It gives certainly just pure joy. But it also takes an intellectual

10:11.880 --> 10:22.831
[SPEAKER_01]: curiosity, or you may call it a need for an intellectual challenge and for problem solving. And I've spent 25 years in the tech industry, which

10:22.891 --> 10:31.856
[SPEAKER_01]: is all about finding opportunities and finding problems to solve and ways to solve those problems. And paragliding gives us here in one

10:31.996 --> 10:39.120
[SPEAKER_01]: flight, if you're doing a cross-country flight over some challenging terrain, it's basically an endless stream of information and of

10:39.360 --> 10:49.645
[SPEAKER_01]: problems to solve. And that never gets old, in my opinion. Now, wow, what an explanation. I think the first thing that comes to my mind is that even

10:49.685 --> 10:56.488
[SPEAKER_03]: though you're Indian by origin, but you're definitely not the typical IT guy, regardless of spending 25 years behind a computer.

10:59.429 --> 11:06.051
[SPEAKER_03]: What a transition, man. What a transition. This is needless to say, this is inspiring and reminds me of a saying called the better late than

11:06.091 --> 11:14.595
[SPEAKER_03]: never. The moment you have a chance in your life to to pursue your meaningful goals. Just go for it. You never know how much fun you've

11:14.615 --> 11:23.360
[SPEAKER_03]: missed out and how much fun there is to be created in time to come. So way to go, man. Great. But another thing that comes to my mind is when you're

11:23.380 --> 11:31.585
[SPEAKER_03]: transitioning from a very fulfilling, stable life onto something as exciting as this, as you said, it's technical, it requires hard work,

11:31.905 --> 11:42.971
[SPEAKER_03]: it's very rewarding. How do you find placing your trust in people while entering a whole new industry? Because obviously, as per the theme of

11:43.011 --> 11:52.176
[SPEAKER_03]: this episode, risk versus reward, for you, somebody who has a fair bit of knowledge how the world works, you've been at the top end of your game in

11:52.216 --> 11:59.320
[SPEAKER_03]: whatever you were doing, be it working in IT, and then you suddenly switch over to a brand new industry, which is a complete, you're a new

11:59.360 --> 12:07.106
[SPEAKER_03]: beginner. So navigating risk versus reward and identifying the correct people in your life and placing trust, how did those kind of

12:07.166 --> 12:13.893
[SPEAKER_03]: questions, how did those challenges come to you and how did you navigate your way around them? Yes, that's a very interesting question. So

12:14.793 --> 12:27.622
[SPEAKER_01]: fundamentally, I, when confronted with any new situation or new domain, I like to rely on multiple sources of information. And there's a

12:27.942 --> 12:34.327
[SPEAKER_01]: saying in science that basically in science, there are no authorities. There's people who know and the papers that are written that are

12:34.347 --> 12:42.434
[SPEAKER_01]: reviewed by others and so on and so forth. But everything and everybody is potentially fallible. And so I just approach things with a certain

12:42.654 --> 12:51.843
[SPEAKER_01]: degree of skepticism. But that is positive skepticism. That's a skepticism... to try and understand as deeply as I can and not just take

12:51.884 --> 13:02.012
[SPEAKER_01]: something as gospel or face value. So I just approach it with that mindset. But that said, concretely, When I started paragliding

13:02.032 --> 13:10.398
[SPEAKER_01]: initially, it was hard. I simply went to the first school that was convenient and close by and learned from them. There were some good

13:10.418 --> 13:21.045
[SPEAKER_01]: things. There were some not so good things. But very quickly, I started, even in that setup, identify who are the pilots who basically have a

13:21.085 --> 13:27.790
[SPEAKER_01]: great reputation, who other pilots think of as good pilots. Certainly, there is some credibility there. and also results. I was very fortunate

13:27.830 --> 13:35.657
[SPEAKER_01]: that a former US national champion was an instructor in this school. And that adds a lot to the credibility. And there is that. And definitely

13:35.817 --> 13:43.403
[SPEAKER_01]: people, good pilots are probably the best source of information if you can identify them. I was super lucky that within the first few months of

13:43.503 --> 13:55.794
[SPEAKER_01]: my starting, I ran into two or three pilots who were not just extremely knowledgeable, but also very helpful. But then I also rely a lot on just

13:55.974 --> 14:04.341
[SPEAKER_01]: books, really. YouTube videos, for sure. They're nice and handy and convenient. But I go back to the books a lot. I have pretty much the entire

14:04.361 --> 14:13.008
[SPEAKER_01]: library of major books that have been written on paragliding, and I've gone through most of them. So these are the kind of primary sources of

14:13.048 --> 14:26.052
[SPEAKER_01]: information. But there's another thing I would like to add, which is, I think very key to progression is a sense of humility, a sense of not being

14:27.033 --> 14:37.414
[SPEAKER_01]: 100% sure of anything myself. Always being open to the idea that I'm carrying in my head is... there's a certain probability of its

14:37.554 --> 14:48.379
[SPEAKER_01]: correctness, but it's potentially incorrect. And so that allows them to keep learning from new information that comes your way rather than

14:48.419 --> 15:01.264
[SPEAKER_01]: just discarding it. And in that sense, I think there is something for me to learn From every pilot out there, every pilot I meet, regardless of

15:01.304 --> 15:09.326
[SPEAKER_01]: their skill or experience, in most cases, something positive to learn. In a few exceptional cases, something not to do. But regardless,

15:09.346 --> 15:17.429
[SPEAKER_01]: there's an opportunity to learn something from everyone. And I just have a desire and a curiosity to find out what that is that I can learn

15:17.469 --> 15:30.712
[SPEAKER_01]: from, from this new engagement and from this new opportunity that I have. And again, I'm not sure if this is super clear, but one thing that

15:30.752 --> 15:42.060
[SPEAKER_01]: has added a lot to my progression that I can recommend highly to people is to try within the limits of your resources to try and fly as many

15:42.200 --> 15:56.289
[SPEAKER_01]: different sites as possible. Because every new site you go to, you meet newer local pilots and some new special situations that a site has that

15:56.309 --> 16:06.593
[SPEAKER_01]: the local pilots will explain to you. And that helps greatly in your overall body of knowledge. And it develops a comfort also so that when

16:06.633 --> 16:16.495
[SPEAKER_01]: you go to the next new site, You know how to assess it based on all your previous learnings. There'll still be something new. But based on your

16:16.515 --> 16:22.659
[SPEAKER_01]: previous learnings, you say, hey, these might be the risks. This might be what happens. And then you correlate it with what the local pilots

16:22.679 --> 16:31.143
[SPEAKER_01]: tell you. And they'll no doubt add something new. But all of this is helping greatly. The difference, initially, you asked me about the

16:31.163 --> 16:39.588
[SPEAKER_01]: number of flying hours. I think the number of flying hours are important. But just as important is the quality of those hours. Were

16:39.608 --> 16:48.312
[SPEAKER_01]: they always doing the same thing at the same site? Or were they doing different things at different sites? And so I think that has been very

16:48.332 --> 16:55.879
[SPEAKER_01]: helpful for me. Got it. I think just to sum it up, the first thing is look at the testimonials, that is, check out the reviews about how trustworthy

16:55.899 --> 17:03.665
[SPEAKER_03]: the place is where you're going and putting all your trust in. And secondly is try and gather as much knowledge as you can from all the open

17:03.685 --> 17:12.491
[SPEAKER_03]: source trustworthy sources. Again, that is the key over here. And the third one is be open to suggestions and be open to learning something.

17:12.811 --> 17:21.336
[SPEAKER_03]: But at the same time, I think I can compare with my progression and my early days that there has to be some sort of barrier where you know when to

17:21.376 --> 17:28.739
[SPEAKER_03]: filter out. And which is, it's a bit too hard to build up this barrier in early days, and especially if you're in a younger age group. Let's say

17:28.759 --> 17:37.502
[SPEAKER_03]: you're a teenage pilot, or you're somebody who does not really know how to navigate the people spectrum, where somebody who's 20 years older to

17:37.562 --> 17:43.764
[SPEAKER_03]: you says that, all right, I've been flying for so many years, I've been teaching. Then, and he comes up with the statement in just because the

17:43.964 --> 17:55.388
[SPEAKER_03]: age gap, you tend to give in to whatever So I think that is a very thin line where you decide whether you want to give your trust 100% to the person at

17:55.408 --> 18:02.870
[SPEAKER_03]: the school stage or in the early instructor stage. After building a bit of experience, of course, it comes easier. So I think that is one thing

18:02.930 --> 18:14.238
[SPEAKER_03]: that people can answer for themselves, probably. Yeah, that's well said, Andy. I agree with that. And just to add to that, though, Just like I

18:14.258 --> 18:23.732
[SPEAKER_01]: said, a new student should pretty much choose a good instructor or a good school and trust them entirely because you're essentially placing

18:23.752 --> 18:31.217
[SPEAKER_01]: your life in that person's hands at that point. And they know better than you. But once you are out of kind of the school stage, you're now an

18:31.237 --> 18:37.382
[SPEAKER_01]: intermediate pilot, which, by the way, most pilots will be for their entire career. I very much consider myself squarely in the

18:37.722 --> 18:43.867
[SPEAKER_01]: intermediate pilot category. There are very few pilots who are advanced pilots. And if anybody has any doubts whether you're an

18:43.887 --> 18:54.815
[SPEAKER_01]: advanced pilot or not, you are an intermediate pilot. So for all of us intermediate pilots, I think of paragliding as an art and a science.

18:55.765 --> 19:05.663
[SPEAKER_01]: There are parts that are pretty much science in terms of rules. Thou shalt or shalt not do. And if you break those rules, you will pay the

19:05.703 --> 19:13.822
[SPEAKER_01]: consequences if not in that flight. in some other flight where it was almost exactly the same situation. It's just that the roll of the dice

19:14.362 --> 19:23.564
[SPEAKER_01]: was in your favor. So those ones are golden. You don't break those. But there's a lot of paragliding that is an art. It's still evolving. It's

19:23.644 --> 19:30.506
[SPEAKER_01]: still not fully understood. And we are very lucky, actually, to be in a sport at that stage of its development, where there's just so much new

19:31.206 --> 19:40.350
[SPEAKER_01]: that pretty much nobody knows. And people are still figuring that out. It's very exciting, actually. And in those areas are the ones I would say

19:40.730 --> 19:48.256
[SPEAKER_01]: I think all of us benefit from keeping an open mind because these are not necessarily safety-related things. These are here just how can you

19:49.097 --> 19:58.745
[SPEAKER_01]: come out better or how can you pick up better glide lines and such. And the risk in those ones is that you might bomb out. Those are not

19:58.845 --> 20:06.508
[SPEAKER_01]: life-threatening, but they help greatly in your progression if you figure those things out. And those, I would say, keep an open mind,

20:07.169 --> 20:14.976
[SPEAKER_01]: experiment, listen to the session of some other pilot, try it. If it works, keep it. If it doesn't, discard it. Or maybe try it again in some

20:15.016 --> 20:25.061
[SPEAKER_01]: other situation. But I think that is pretty, is very important. That's pure words of wisdom, Subhir. I think that you've put it very, very well,

20:25.461 --> 20:33.748
[SPEAKER_03]: that there are some rules, non-negotiable, stick to them, or consequences will be grave and hard. The other ones, the more you work

20:33.788 --> 20:43.250
[SPEAKER_03]: with them, the better the experience gets. Nice. Great. But Sabir, as you said, you had a, I forgot the term that you used, but you had a very

20:43.430 --> 20:54.193
[SPEAKER_03]: subtle urgency when you began this sport finally and you were getting into it. So how did you plan out your hard work? What was your

20:54.233 --> 21:02.495
[SPEAKER_03]: visualization like? Did you manifest this for a while or was it just after the tandem that it happened? How did hard work look like in your

21:02.515 --> 21:10.581
[SPEAKER_03]: flying career? Yeah, so there are a number of things I would say. Suddenly the bug bit me after the tandem flight that I mentioned. And as I

21:10.621 --> 21:18.523
[SPEAKER_01]: went back to school and did a little bit of ground handling and then my first flight, that was instant love. This is just amazing. I just want to

21:18.563 --> 21:27.606
[SPEAKER_01]: do more of it. And I want to do it well. I would say there is something in me which says, If it's worth doing something, then it's worth doing it.

21:27.786 --> 21:37.191
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no doing something half-heartedly for me. And I really wanted to become a competent and good pilot. And one of my early inspirations

21:37.251 --> 21:46.575
[SPEAKER_01]: was a friend and a mentor of mine, Mitch Riley. He's, like I mentioned, a former US Open national champion and has also done the X-Alps, I

21:46.595 --> 21:57.858
[SPEAKER_01]: believe, in 2016. And a few things he told me, I just took to heart. And one of those was, that there is no such thing as too much ground handling.

21:58.519 --> 22:10.068
