[SPEAKER_03]: course, the more passionate you are, the more further you raise the bar for defining too much for you. But let's say when it came to balancing the

24:01.895 --> 24:08.942
[SPEAKER_03]: mental part of the hard work, the physical part of the hard work, resting in between, I understand you fixed a lot of everything else in life so

24:08.962 --> 24:17.229
[SPEAKER_03]: that you could be in a position to push this that hard. But did you ever face the challenge of having an imbalance in between them where you were

24:17.249 --> 24:27.462
[SPEAKER_03]: like, oh my God, I'm pushing too hard and maybe I need to figure out this? How did you navigate those kind of days? So I would say while there might

24:27.502 --> 24:35.911
[SPEAKER_01]: be some truth that extremes unhealthy, for me personally, I don't think I've reached that point yet because there's another parallel part. I

24:35.931 --> 24:43.959
[SPEAKER_01]: forget the name of the author who basically says for somebody to become an expert at anything takes about 10,000 hours of doing that thing. You

24:43.979 --> 24:53.085
[SPEAKER_01]: might have that. And right now I'm only at 1,500 hours and 8,500 more hours is going to take me a long time. Till then, I'm not a quote unquote

24:53.245 --> 25:02.852
[SPEAKER_01]: expert. And so I feel, and another thing is I have seven years of life experience behind me, like you mentioned, but I'm only at three, three

25:02.892 --> 25:12.998
[SPEAKER_01]: and a half years of flying. And so I'm still in that early stage of this is exciting. This is new. This is wonderful. And there is more for me to

25:13.078 --> 25:23.384
[SPEAKER_01]: learn than I have learned so far. In fact, orders of magnitude more for me to learn. And that keeps it fresh. That keeps it exciting. And just to

25:23.404 --> 25:30.088
[SPEAKER_01]: give you a concrete example, I have a background in adventure sports in general. I'm a mountaineer. I'm a skier. I just love being in the

25:30.128 --> 25:38.272
[SPEAKER_01]: mountains. I've done that for many years. And so there was a natural attraction for mountains for me. And so as I started paragliding, the

25:38.312 --> 25:50.277
[SPEAKER_01]: natural thing for me was hike and fly. I just did from, it was my happiest hiking a new site, a new mountain and just flying off it. And but but that

25:50.297 --> 25:58.224
[SPEAKER_01]: got a little old after a while because you spend only so much time in the air. And as I got hungry for more and more airtime, I switched to more

25:58.445 --> 26:08.553
[SPEAKER_01]: cross-country flying. And then as I became slightly better at that, I started doing competition flying. And now, you know, my interest in

26:08.613 --> 26:16.737
[SPEAKER_01]: combination flying is actually waning a little bit. And I'm super passionate about my new interest, which is acro, of which I know

26:16.857 --> 26:25.741
[SPEAKER_01]: absolutely nothing. And I'm going to start an acro progression this October in Olodunis. So it's new, it's exciting, it's different, and it

26:25.841 --> 26:36.062
[SPEAKER_01]: is energizing. Way to go, man. All right. Watch out, acro boy. Subir is coming for you. with all his heart. But I just want to backtrack a tiny bit

26:36.102 --> 26:45.804
[SPEAKER_03]: here to Malcolm Gladwell, if I'm not mistaken, is the author who mentioned the 10,000-hour theory. And he very clearly says that 10,000

26:46.084 --> 26:54.566
[SPEAKER_03]: hours are really effective if you are being watched by a coach and your mistakes are being picked out so that you follow those 10,000 hours in a

26:54.606 --> 27:01.769
[SPEAKER_03]: certain way, like you don't go off track. So when you have been chasing all this airtime and you have been pushing your limits and trying to

27:01.969 --> 27:10.295
[SPEAKER_03]: become a better version of yourself in the air, have you had a chance to be mentored or being coached or being watched upon by someone? And if yes,

27:10.355 --> 27:23.288
[SPEAKER_03]: then if you can talk a little bit about that side of your journey. 100%. I think Maxwell Gladwell said it. Yes, I think Malcolm Gladwell is spot

27:23.408 --> 27:31.210
[SPEAKER_01]: on. He's 100% correct because there's a saying in golf, for example, that if you practice a lot of the wrong thing, you become great at the

27:31.250 --> 27:42.152
[SPEAKER_01]: wrong thing. And so it's absolutely true. And I do have a number of mentors who have guided me from time to time and who still do. There is one

27:42.192 --> 27:54.805
[SPEAKER_01]: more thing, though. that nobody is going to be watching you all the time, except you yourself. You are watching your flying all the time. And you

27:55.085 --> 28:04.691
[SPEAKER_01]: have to be your first and best critic. Anytime anything happens that catches you that, hey, that wasn't right, and a slightly bad takeoff,

28:05.651 --> 28:15.913
[SPEAKER_01]: something going sideways in the air, you have to... not just brush it off saying, yeah, that's okay, let me just move on. You have to first

28:15.973 --> 28:23.936
[SPEAKER_01]: acknowledge that I made a mistake. And secondly, try to figure out what that mistake was. And suddenly your body of knowledge might not be

28:24.236 --> 28:33.515
[SPEAKER_01]: enough To understand that, and at that point, you go to your friends, your advisors, your mentors, or any pilot who is further along in their

28:33.535 --> 28:42.378
[SPEAKER_01]: progression than you are, and you review your situation with them and ask them for perhaps what has happened. If you have video footage, then

28:42.698 --> 28:50.060
[SPEAKER_01]: that is very helpful. But even otherwise, just talking through that incident is extremely important. And I would go back to this flight log

28:50.080 --> 29:00.481
[SPEAKER_01]: comment that I mentioned earlier. One of the reasons your flight log is so important is that when you fill your flight log entry at the end of your

29:00.581 --> 29:11.605
[SPEAKER_01]: flight or at the end of the day, you try to at least identify some mistake that you made that you could have done better or some other insight that

29:11.665 --> 29:24.271
[SPEAKER_01]: you have from that flight. Because if you... You won't learn something new from every flight than while that flight was fun and joyful and for

29:24.331 --> 29:32.476
[SPEAKER_01]: sure. And those are good enough reasons to do that flight. From a pure progression perspective, that flight was not helpful. And so I think

29:33.517 --> 29:43.541
[SPEAKER_01]: the discipline of maintaining a flight log just allows you to reflect on your flight and put something into words that of what you're learning

29:43.941 --> 29:53.766
[SPEAKER_01]: was. And that I think is extremely helpful. So you have to be your first and best critic, I would say in a nutshell. Just be true to yourself and

29:53.786 --> 30:02.351
[SPEAKER_03]: stay disciplined. And these are like two of the foolproof ways of progressing safely and on your terms, I think. Yes. And again, I would

30:02.371 --> 30:10.555
[SPEAKER_01]: say not to brush off something. Let's say you pulled up your wing, the wing didn't come up right, and you still forced a launch and you got away

30:10.576 --> 30:19.812
[SPEAKER_01]: with it. The lesson from that shouldn't be, ah, that's all okay. I got away with it. It's fine. No. The outcome should not determine whether

30:19.852 --> 30:27.516
[SPEAKER_01]: there was a learning opportunity or not. Because you do the exact same thing another time, some other little variable will be bad enough that

30:28.156 --> 30:37.561
[SPEAKER_01]: you might not have the successful launch, for example. And so we have to learn not from the outcome of incidents, but from the fact that an

30:37.762 --> 30:45.626
[SPEAKER_01]: incident happened, even if the outcome was favorable, and try to understand what happened, why did it happen, did I know something was

30:45.666 --> 30:54.008
[SPEAKER_01]: going wrong, and I still... made a poor choice, in which case I need to whack myself on the wrist and not do it again? Or do I not understand what

30:54.048 --> 31:02.834
[SPEAKER_01]: happened, in which case I need to go and talk to another pilot to understand what might have happened? And so this is key. Got it. I think

31:02.874 --> 31:10.545
[SPEAKER_03]: that just makes it very simple for our listeners to understand how to make sure that you stay on the good side and the more fun side of the sport

31:11.386 --> 31:19.396
[SPEAKER_03]: as and when you wish or take to the skies every now and then. But while we're talking about staying safe and making it count every time. You

31:19.416 --> 31:26.782
[SPEAKER_03]: said you have done a ton of SIVs. Can you just brush us, can you just tell our listeners the role of safety training and acrobatics in flying in

31:26.843 --> 31:34.749
[SPEAKER_03]: general? Because I've heard, I don't know if this is a perception or not, but majority of good pilots who we see on Instagram and Facebook, they

31:34.829 --> 31:44.236
[SPEAKER_03]: have an acro background and everybody keeps on saying, do SIVs, do keep on doing all of that. But Money is a big thing in this sport. And as a pilot,

31:44.316 --> 31:52.441
[SPEAKER_03]: you practically cannot afford to keep on going to SIV clinics. You'll burn a hole in your pocket and you'll eventually give up. So how do you

31:52.521 --> 32:02.827
[SPEAKER_03]: navigate that side of the sport where you are not being watched in every collapse? You just have to take your glider up on a lake and do those

32:02.887 --> 32:13.496
[SPEAKER_03]: things yourself. And of course, you want to be the safest pilot, but yet safety training in acrobatics is an essential part to the progression.

32:13.936 --> 32:22.949
[SPEAKER_03]: So how did you navigate this and what advice would you give to every other pilot who's trying to follow your footsteps? Yeah, my assessment is

32:23.009 --> 32:35.072
[SPEAKER_01]: that SIV is intrinsic to safe flying. Before an incident happens in the wild, if you have done an SIV or a few and have a better understanding of

32:35.092 --> 32:47.154
[SPEAKER_01]: handling more situations, you will just have better outcomes. And I would advocate pilots who are flying and have aspired to fly

32:47.654 --> 32:55.324
[SPEAKER_01]: cross-country to do an SIV more early in the progression. Now, see, But at the same time, I wouldn't say there's kind of one-size-fits-all.

32:56.165 --> 33:05.954
[SPEAKER_01]: There might be pilots who just do coastal soaring on a soaring site with laminar winds. And that's all they want to do. And that's fantastic.

33:05.974 --> 33:14.622
[SPEAKER_01]: There are other pilots who might just want to occasionally fly their home site in gentle conditions, morning, evenings, just to enjoy some

33:14.702 --> 33:24.744
[SPEAKER_01]: airtime. They're busy with their lives. They have other priorities. I think it's okay for those pilots to fly safely in known good conditions

33:25.785 --> 33:36.914
[SPEAKER_01]: to manage that risk. But any pilot who's thermal flying and is flying cross-country, you are essentially seeking turbulent air. We're all

33:37.614 --> 33:46.841
[SPEAKER_01]: naturally afraid of turbulence, but guess what a thermal is? A thermal, by definition, is turbulence. And we are actively seeking turbulence

33:46.921 --> 33:53.290
[SPEAKER_01]: and putting our glider into turbulent air. And this thing about adventure, that sometimes you go looking for adventure and you find it.

33:54.128 --> 34:03.830
[SPEAKER_01]: and occasionally you might find this really rough air and your glider goes sideways and when it does you can reliably recover a glider only

34:03.950 --> 34:10.731
[SPEAKER_01]: from situations that you have seen before if your glider is in a configuration that you've never seen before your chances of

34:10.771 --> 34:20.213
[SPEAKER_01]: recovering from that are pretty low actually and your best bet is to throw your reserve and we can talk about that as a subtopic down the road

34:20.233 --> 34:30.476
[SPEAKER_01]: but The point is, if you're flying in turbulent air, you should have put your wing into different configurations and recovered from them. And

34:30.496 --> 34:39.702
[SPEAKER_01]: that's really what an SIV is. And for a pilot flying thermal or cross-country, I would say within the first probably 50 to 100 hours of

34:39.742 --> 34:49.968
[SPEAKER_01]: flying, I would recommend they do an SIV. And for pilots who've never done an SIV before, For most pilots, there are exceptions, but for most

34:50.008 --> 34:58.977
[SPEAKER_01]: pilots, the experience is going to be borderline terrifying. For some, it's just fun. But for most, there is some degree of panic involved.

35:01.920 --> 35:11.385
[SPEAKER_01]: yourself not so much reliable do you have the ability to calm yourself assess and give the proper input at the proper time and so on so forth and

35:11.485 --> 35:21.151
[SPEAKER_01]: you just have to go through your first siv to get a better sense of that and it's in your second siv that you can really learn a lot in my opinion and

35:21.191 --> 35:29.555
[SPEAKER_01]: that was certainly my experience but i've spoken to other pilots too And so I would basically advocate the first SIV in 50 to 100 hours and the

35:29.615 --> 35:38.059
[SPEAKER_01]: second SIV as soon thereafter as possible, not wait for the next year. And that's just the price of admission, in my opinion, for thermal

35:38.079 --> 35:50.798
[SPEAKER_01]: flying. Gliders are expensive. Gliders and full kits cost, we know what they cost. But proper training... is, I think, incredibly efficient in

35:50.858 --> 35:59.899
[SPEAKER_01]: terms of how well you and how confidently you then fly those tools. And rather than investing in the next, this topic never comes up, in my

35:59.939 --> 36:07.661
[SPEAKER_01]: opinion, for most pilots. They'll buy a wing or two, then they want to, at some point, many pilots progress to the next wing. But that next wing is

36:07.681 --> 36:15.703
[SPEAKER_01]: going to be very expensive. That SIV and other training, let's say a cross-country clinic, is less expensive than a new wing. And it will

36:15.763 --> 36:25.313
[SPEAKER_01]: help you fly whatever wing you're flying today, an A, a B, or a C, much, much better than spending the money to buy your next level wing.

36:25.653 --> 36:32.881
[SPEAKER_01]: Performance doesn't come from the wing. Performance comes from the expertise of the pilot. And the more you invest in your own expertise,

36:33.401 --> 36:40.828
[SPEAKER_01]: the better your performance will become. That makes total sense to me. I think that's the easiest way to put it. I think it reminds me of a saying,

36:40.868 --> 36:47.991
[SPEAKER_03]: the more you bleed and practice, the less you breathe in water or something like that. So when it comes to navigating the risk versus

36:48.011 --> 36:56.935
[SPEAKER_03]: reward and glider handling and all of this, I can share my experience. I finally upgraded to a two-liner and I love pushing the bar. I wouldn't

36:56.975 --> 37:03.498
[SPEAKER_03]: say that it comes out as intuitive, but it's something that I'm very comfortable with. But then the moment I enter turbulent air and

37:03.858 --> 37:11.303
[SPEAKER_03]: controlling the wing with brakes is much more reassuring than controlling the wing with Bs. Now, the point is, where do I draw a line

37:11.403 --> 37:22.213
[SPEAKER_03]: that at this level of turbulence or this level of movement, these are no longer my comfort zone? Because you can be mentally very strong. You can

37:22.253 --> 37:30.678
[SPEAKER_03]: be all motivated to go out and push the limits. But then SIVs will answer a lot of these questions, I completely agree. But there are some times

37:30.718 --> 37:38.360
[SPEAKER_03]: like we see best of the best pilots, even triple C wings get collapses in big gaggles and unfortunately some of them have to pay a pretty heavy

37:38.420 --> 37:44.362
[SPEAKER_03]: price for it. Of course there's a level of uncertainty that we will never be able to define because the sport is still developing, there's not

37:44.402 --> 37:51.951
[SPEAKER_03]: enough data and all the other reasons. But how have you managed answering this question for yourself that, all right, now I need to calm

37:52.011 --> 38:02.243
[SPEAKER_03]: down. This is going beyond my skill and comfort. And this is where I reduce my reward because the risk is too high. Yes, there are a number of

38:02.423 --> 38:10.326
[SPEAKER_01]: things in what you've said, and they were all very important. I'll just address your last question first to give a concrete example. Like I

38:10.366 --> 38:20.530
[SPEAKER_01]: said, I invested in training, did SIVs, and I progressed through all the wing classes. One thing that I realized as I was progressing and also

38:20.570 --> 38:29.115
[SPEAKER_01]: talking to really experienced pilots was, I think, the answer to the question, when is it right for a pilot to step up to the next wing class?

38:29.196 --> 38:37.021
[SPEAKER_01]: This is a very common question. And there are many considerations. Again, we are talking about cross-country flying here, or competition

38:37.041 --> 38:47.668
[SPEAKER_01]: flying perhaps, not acro or anything else. So for cross- country flying, the answer to that question basically boils down to, you can

38:47.768 --> 38:59.675
[SPEAKER_01]: potentially step up to the next wing class if you are comfortable flying the full speed range of your current glider it's as simple as that and

38:59.715 --> 39:11.046
[SPEAKER_01]: your full speed range is not from trim but from min sync all the way to full bar this is the speed range of your glider if you're not comfortable

39:11.926 --> 39:22.330
[SPEAKER_01]: flying this glide your current glider in this entire speed range you will not benefit from stepping up to the next wing class because it's not

39:22.390 --> 39:35.315
[SPEAKER_01]: the wing that gives you performance, like I said. The thing is, within reason, most gliders from pretty much a low B all the way to, for sure, a D

39:35.355 --> 39:45.317
[SPEAKER_01]: or even a triple C, at trim, they all vary between 38 to 40 kilometers per hour. They're all about the same speed. The performance of the glider

39:45.337 --> 39:53.603
[SPEAKER_01]: class comes from bar, as we know, and particularly from the polar curve and what happens to the glider when you're pushing full bar. If you're

39:53.623 --> 40:01.968
[SPEAKER_01]: not going to be comfortable flying the glider on full bar and also know the stall point and fly the glider at min sync safely, you should focus on

40:02.249 --> 40:13.030
[SPEAKER_01]: learning to fly your existing glider better. That's the bottom line. So for me, personally, I was comfortable all the way to D. I flew the Zeolite

40:13.190 --> 40:23.716
[SPEAKER_01]: and then the Xeno. And I was totally comfortable. I have about 700 hours on two different Xenos and two different sizes. And then when I stepped

40:23.776 --> 40:34.703
[SPEAKER_01]: up to a triple C wing, I could see that I'm immediately not confident. And I'm not confident on full bar in the same turbulent air that I was

40:34.763 --> 40:47.268
[SPEAKER_01]: confident on my Xeno. And I was like, this is not comfortable. This is not safe. And I don't want to be doing this. And I asked one of my mentors this

40:47.329 --> 40:57.156
[SPEAKER_01]: question. Hey, how do I overcome this? Should I spend more hours flying my D glider? Or should I just deal with it and become more comfortable

40:57.256 --> 41:06.484
[SPEAKER_01]: flying the Enzo in perhaps calmer conditions first and so on and so forth? And he said, neither. Neither will be helpful. What will be

41:06.504 --> 41:16.203
[SPEAKER_01]: helpful is acrobatics. And that, my friend, is one of the reasons why I'm starting Acro. Not because it particularly excites or interests me

41:16.223 --> 41:24.307
[SPEAKER_00]: that I want to be doing all these cool tricks, which might well end up being the case. I don't know as yet. But because I want to become better at

41:24.487 --> 41:33.492
[SPEAKER_01]: wing control. And what Evan told me was, it's not that, and again, I don't know for sure. I have an open mind about this. But he said it's not about

41:34.092 --> 41:42.296
[SPEAKER_01]: the tricks themselves that how to do those tricks that make you better at wing control. It's the process of learning acro. You put your glider in

41:42.396 --> 41:51.426
[SPEAKER_01]: all kinds of shapes and situations and learn to deal with them and to a certain extent anticipate that they're about to happen. And that is the

41:51.466 --> 41:59.565
[SPEAKER_01]: training that you need to fly a really hot glider comfortably. That makes total sense. As you mentioned earlier, that if you have seen your

41:59.605 --> 42:05.710
[SPEAKER_03]: wing in that configuration, the chances of you recovering it are much more higher than you being caught up by surprise and just being taken

42:05.750 --> 42:15.118
[SPEAKER_03]: aback by what's going on. And I think that is one of the reasons why other people are also good acropilots. Absolutely, Andy. I did want to touch

42:15.178 --> 42:22.610
[SPEAKER_01]: on one more topic that you brought up in this question, which was being comfortable on brakes versus comfortable on the rear risers. Now,

42:22.630 --> 42:32.657
[SPEAKER_01]: two-liners are special beasts because they are very direct. When you press more bar, your angle of attack becomes lower, the glider speeds

42:32.737 --> 42:42.761
[SPEAKER_01]: up. And if you hold that bar and you pull the risers without distorting the shape of the wing, your angle of attack becomes greater. and your

42:42.781 --> 42:50.645
[SPEAKER_01]: speed reduces. And so to a certain extent, if you're at trim, and after that you push half bar and you pull the risers an equivalent amount, your

42:50.665 --> 43:00.471
[SPEAKER_01]: glider is exactly at trim in configuration and in speed. And so this direct handling is extremely useful, and developing a comfort for it is

43:00.631 --> 43:07.575
[SPEAKER_01]: extremely handy. Because when you pull the brakes, it's actually distorting the shape of the wing. It's steering performance. The whole

43:07.615 --> 43:15.100
[SPEAKER_01]: reason you're flying a hot glider, a two-liner C, and so on and so forth is because you want that performance. And it's the risers, using the

43:15.120 --> 43:23.168
[SPEAKER_01]: risers that give you control without sacrificing performance. And it's just phenomenal. And you do get comfort with it as you keep using

43:23.208 --> 43:33.436
[SPEAKER_01]: them more. Like I said, I have 700 hours on the Xeno, another 150 hours on a Zeolite. In all of that, At this point, I am actually, it might sound

43:33.476 --> 43:43.122
[SPEAKER_02]: strange, more comfortable on the risers simply because I've spent so much time on them than I am even with the brakes. That makes sense. But for

43:43.142 --> 43:49.406
[SPEAKER_03]: people who have never flown a two-liner, pulling risers on a three-liner is a whole different world. It takes shitloads of energy.

43:49.826 --> 43:58.772
[SPEAKER_03]: The profile does not distort that much. But do you ever get tired? I've seen your flight logs. You fly for hours and hours. So two-liner flying

43:58.832 --> 44:08.133
[SPEAKER_03]: and rear riser control, does it tire you out more muscularly, not mentally? It does. It does. There is more pressure on at least the two

44:08.153 --> 44:18.617
[SPEAKER_01]: liners that I fly. Keep in mind that wings are different. There are some two liners in particular where there is less pressure on the rear

44:18.637 --> 44:28.281
[SPEAKER_01]: risers. But still, it's more than brakes typically. Yeah, so it does get tiring. But this brings us to another topic of flying endurance.

44:29.021 --> 44:39.429
[SPEAKER_01]: Endurance flying, I think, is something that needs to be developed. both mentally as well as physically. And for a cross-country pilot who

44:39.449 --> 44:51.260
[SPEAKER_01]: wants to fly far, it's a good area to invest in. And for the simple reason, and again, I'm not saying that everybody should aspire to fly great long

44:51.300 --> 44:59.004
[SPEAKER_01]: distances. But if you do aspire, if you do want to do 100 kilometer flights or 200 kilometer flights, for example, Entirely your choice.

44:59.224 --> 45:06.731
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you want to do that, then you have to do certain things to achieve those goals. And one of them is to be able to fly long. The simple math is

45:07.552 --> 45:17.421
[SPEAKER_01]: that a typical intermediate pilot flying a B or a C wing will have an average cross-country speed of, on a decently good day, 20 to 25

45:17.661 --> 45:25.462
[SPEAKER_01]: kilometers per hour. including tumbling and all of that. And so if you've got to fly 100K, you'll be flying for four or five hours. It is, in

45:25.502 --> 45:35.786
[SPEAKER_01]: my opinion, much better for pilots at their local sites, even if they are not conducive to flying long distances, but they work for a long enough

45:35.806 --> 45:44.870
[SPEAKER_01]: duration to launch and just fly for two hours if they fly for an hour or fly for three hours, four hours, five hours, doing essentially nothing but

45:44.950 --> 45:51.913
[SPEAKER_01]: tumbling up, bring overs down, or if you're lucky enough to have at least a couple of features and a valley crossing to just do the valley

45:51.933 --> 45:59.158
[SPEAKER_01]: crossings back and forth, thermal up, understand thermals, just spend a great deal of time in the air because there's no substitute for airtime

45:59.438 --> 46:08.123
[SPEAKER_01]: just for learning, but also to build endurance. And so if you're super comfortable flying for four or five hours when you launch, it can be

46:08.163 --> 46:16.526
[SPEAKER_01]: mentally exhausting, like you said. Then when the day is there and you are at a site where 100K is possible, You don't have to worry about the

46:16.566 --> 46:23.293
[SPEAKER_01]: flying for five hours a bit. You just have to worry about terrain and the thermals. And so that just comes with practice. It's as simple as that.

46:23.453 --> 46:33.523
[SPEAKER_01]: There's no better way to build arm strength, I think, than just by doing the activity that you like. You could certainly go to the gym and so on and

46:33.543 --> 46:42.773
[SPEAKER_02]: so forth, but I mostly just fly. Indeed, and you're pretty good at it as well. So these are golden pieces of advice that you have given out to our

46:42.813 --> 46:49.698
[SPEAKER_03]: community here and to all the audiences. But before I move on to the last part of the show, there was one topic that I feel we really need to

